WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.078
Local language-model performance combines responsiveness, throughput, quality, stability, and usability.

00:00:05.078 --> 00:00:15.015
Measure time to first token, prompt processing, generation speed, end-to-end completion, memory use, thermals, and errors on a repeatable real workload.

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:21.418
A single peak number cannot describe whether the system serves its user well.

00:00:21.418 --> 00:00:23.847
Find the actual bottleneck.

00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:32.016
VRAM, GPU bandwidth, CPU, system RAM, storage, PCI Express transfers, cooling, and power can constrain different phases.

00:00:32.016 --> 00:00:37.978
Choose a model and quantization that leave headroom for context and cache.

00:00:37.978 --> 00:00:47.031
A smaller fully accelerated model often outperforms a larger model split across slow resources while remaining easier to operate.

00:00:47.031 --> 00:00:49.901
Manage context as a resource.

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:55.421
Send relevant, well-scoped prompts and retrieval results instead of maximizing length.

00:00:55.421 --> 00:01:02.708
Tune offload, batch, threads, cache, and backend against the baseline, one variable at a time.

00:01:02.708 --> 00:01:12.202
Select a runtime because it supports the hardware, model format, and workflow reliably, not because it exposes the most complexity.

00:01:12.202 --> 00:01:16.397
Validate changes through repeated tests and sustained runs.

00:01:16.397 --> 00:01:21.917
Account for warm caches, background processes, power modes, and thermal throttling.

00:01:21.917 --> 00:01:26.113
Track known-good drivers and runtimes and preserve rollback.

00:01:26.113 --> 00:01:31.191
The best configuration is not necessarily the fastest isolated benchmark.

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:42.010
It delivers acceptable quality at usable speed, behaves predictably through the real workload, and remains understandable and maintainable when software or requirements change.
